    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so in the ac version, the current isn't actually flowing \"through\" the dielectric material itself, right? it's just the displacement current? could u explain that part a bit more clearly? i'm stuck on the actual mechanism of how the charges move back and forth.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct; the capacitor itself does not conduct current *through* the insulating dielectric. Instead, the current flows around the capacitor by the movement of charge accumulating on its plates, creating a changing electric flux, which is the displacement current. This time-varying electric field passes through the dielectric, effectively allowing the AC signal to propagate. The back-and-forth motion is the charge separation at the plates alternating in time as the AC voltage cycles.",
      "source": "target",
