    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so if the displacement current is just this field changing in the dielectric, how does the frequency affect it? like if i increase the frequency, what's actually happening to the 'resistance' or reactance the capacitor provides? i'm having trouble visualizing why a higher frequency makes the reactance lower.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Increasing the frequency means the voltage is changing its direction much faster, demanding the capacitor to charge and discharge more rapidly. At higher frequencies, the capacitor doesn't have enough time to fully build up a static charge, so the voltage across it remains smaller compared to the source voltage. Consequently, the opposition, or capacitive reactance ($X_C$), decreases because the capacitor acts more like a short circuit at very high frequencies.",
      "source": "target",
